<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crusade of Varna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1443">1443</a>–<a href="/years/1444">1444</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a> was a campaign of 1443–1444 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, Wallachia and others, led by King Władysław III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a>. After the successful 'Long Campaign' of 1443 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-szeged">Peace of Szeged</a>, the crusaders renewed the war and were crushed at Varna in November 1444, where Władysław was killed, ending hopes of driving the Ottomans from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crusade-of-varna.jpg" alt="Crusade of Varna" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Matejko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Varna_1444.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After 1430 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> returned to their aim of controlling all lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> began raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> in 1432, and the attacks intensified after King Sigismund died in 1437. In 1439 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a> capitulated and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> became an Ottoman province, and Despot Đurađ Branković fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. In 1440 Murad besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> but failed to take it. Hungary meanwhile fell into civil war over the succession, and Władysław of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was crowned king on 17 July 1440. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> won him the position of nádor of Transylvania, with responsibility for the southern border, and defeated Ottoman raids in 1441 and 1442. The Church had long urged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a> published a crusading bull on 1 January 1443.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>War was proclaimed on Palm Sunday 1443 at the diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>. With an army of 40,000, mostly Hungarians, Władysław and Hunyadi crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and took Niš and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>. The crusaders expected that Murad's fief-holding cavalry, who had to gather the harvest to pay taxes, could not mobilise quickly, and Hunyadi's winter experience and better armour added to their advantage. They won at Niš and Aleksinac but were defeated at Zlatitsa Pass on 12 December 1443. On the march home they ambushed a pursuing force at Kunovica and took Mahmud Bey prisoner, so that they returned to Buda in February 1444 as heroes, and the king and Church spread word of victories and contradicted anyone who mentioned the loss.</p><p>Murad, pressed by his wife Mara, daughter of Branković, and by the sister of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, sought peace. Talks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szeged">Szeged</a> ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-szeged">Peace of Szeged</a>, sworn on 15 August 1444, though Cardinal Julian Cesarini pressed for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> to continue. Soon after the treaty's requirements were met, Władysław gathered an army of 16,000 men, mostly Hungarian regular troops with forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, joined by 4,000 Wallachians at Nicopolis. Murad, who had retired, came back to lead his army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 10 November 1444 the armies met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> won a decisive victory despite heavy losses, and the crusaders lost King Władysław and over 15,000 men. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> fell back into civil war until Hunyadi was elected regent for the infant Ladislaus in June 1446. Together with the Ottoman victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-kosovo">Second Battle of Kosovo</a> in 1448, Varna deterred European states from sending substantial help to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> before the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453. Hunyadi's victory at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1456 halted Ottoman expansion beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> for seventy years, until the Hungarian army was crushed at Mohács in 1526.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Crusade of Varna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King Władysław III of Poland and Hungary was killed, and the defeat ended the <b>Crusade of Varna</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-10">10 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
